problem_statement.body:
## Problem

The DirectX backend forwards canonical Metal math calls without applying the target calling convention. In particular, Metal/CrossGL `select(falseValue, trueValue, condition)` becomes an HLSL call with the same argument order, although HLSL expects `select(condition, trueValue, falseValue)`.

This can produce valid DXIL with incorrect results, not just a compilation error.

## Reproduction

At translator revision `d88c9121387e918af208814761c6b3d5dc2d2986`, translate this CrossGL shader to DirectX and compile with DXC using `-WX -T cs_6_6 -E CSMain`:

```cgl
shader Math {
    StructuredBuffer<float> values @ binding(1);
    RWStructuredBuffer<float> result @ binding(0);
    compute {
        layout(local_size_x = 1) in;
        void main() {
            result[0] = select(values[0], values[1], false);
        }
    }
}
```

DXC succeeds without diagnostics. The emitted DXIL compares `values[0]` with zero and chooses between `values[1]` and `0.0`; the source must always select `values[0]`.

Replacing that expression with `fmax(fabs(-3.0), fmin(4.0, 5.0))` fails native compilation because `fabs` and `fmin` remain undeclared HLSL callees. These operations reach canonical code from the current MLX complex-power helpers at commit `d9add9d11f3154111a4c85f267ec2fd307ecd18e`.

## Required Behavior

- Lower canonical math operations to the matching HLSL intrinsics after preserving source-defined helper ownership.
- Reorder Boolean scalar/vector selection without introducing short-circuit evaluation of either value argument.
- Retain result types for nested selection and math calls.
- Diagnose unresolved condition types and unsupported integer-mask selection instead of guessing a Boolean conversion.
- Do not redirect an alias to a source-defined `abs`, `min`, or `max` with different semantics.

## Validation

Add warning-fatal native compiler regressions and required Windows numerical readbacks covering both selection branches, vector masks, eager evaluation, finite values, NaNs, and infinities. Keep compiler acceptance separate from numerical correctness and whole-repository runtime integration.

This is a target-lowering gap distinct from the qualified `copysign`/`rint` work in #1776: the operation names here already reach the canonical representation.

